// The event a <webview> dispatches when the page inside it asks for a window
// of its own — a link with target="_blank", a window.open.
// See docs/architecture/ENGINE-FORK.md in the Domicile repository.
//
// Not a web standard and not proposed as one; it exists in Domicile's engine
// fork only, beside the element that dispatches it.
//
// NO CONSTRUCTOR AND NO EVENT-INIT DICTIONARY, unlike the fork's
// control-channel events. Only the browser process ever asks for one of these,
// through HTMLWebViewElement, and a dictionary would be two more generated
// files in three more build lists for a constructor no shell calls. A shell
// that wants to synthesize one in a test dispatches an Event with a `url` on
// it, which is what `BrowserWindow.test.tsx` does.
[
    RuntimeEnabled=DomicileExternalSurface,
    Exposed=Window
] interface DomicileNewWindowEvent : Event {
  // The address the page asked to open, absolute — resolved in the browser
  // process against the document that asked, before this event existed.
  //
  // A SHELL POINTS A SECOND <webview> AT IT, in a second browser window it
  // draws. Nothing in either process has opened a window: the browser refuses
  // the one a guest's page asks for, because a guest has no SiteInstance of its
  // own and content CHECKs that pair. See
  // components/domicile/browser/web_view_guest.h, which also says what refusing
  // costs — the opener, window.open's handle, and a POST body do not survive.
  readonly attribute USVString url;
};
